	doi = {10.1103/PhysRevA.86.013635},
	abstract = {We show that a moving obstacle, in the form of an elongated paddle, can create vortices that are dispersed or induce clusters of like-signed vortices in two-dimensional (2D) Bose-Einstein condensates. We propose statistical measures of clustering based on Ripley's K function which are suitable to the small size and small number of vortices in atomic condensates, which lack the huge number of length scales excited in larger classical and quantum turbulent fluid systems. The evolution and decay of clustering is analyzed using these measures. Experimentally it should prove possible to create such an obstacle by a laser beam and a moving optical mask. The theoretical techniques we present are accessible to experimentalists and extend the current methods available to induce 2D quantum turbulence in Bose-Einstein condensates.},
