	abstract = {This chapter reviews the properties of quantum crystals in which they manifest their unusual nature associated with quantum delocalization of particles. Theoretical and experimental results obtained show that objects considerably different from ordinary crystals are discussed in the chapter. The pair of impurities behaves like a peculiar two-dimensional quasi-particle, which moves as a free particle but only in the crystal hexagonal plane. The vacancy-induced motion of atoms in a crystal is of special interest. The vacancy-induced diffusion of impurities is determined by the inelastic scattering probability. The measurement of internal friction is the most suitable method for the observation of the specific features in question concerning the dislocation behavior. A specific mechanism of uniform relaxation of a non-equilibrium distribution of defects over quasi-momenta exists with the mechanism of relaxation of a non-uniform spatial distribution of defects characteristic of ordinary crystals.},
