	abstract = {Simulations of spatially correlated ballistic deposition have been carried out using simple two- and three-dimensional lattice models. In these models particles are deposited, one at a time, from the ends of the steps in a Levy flight taking place in a line or plane above the surface of the deposit. For deposition on a line, the exponent $\alpha$ that describes the dependence of $\xi$ (the surface width) on the lateral system size (L) is given by $\alpha$=(1/2 for f<=1/2 and $\alpha$=(1/2+(f- 1) / 2 )/3 for (1/2<=f<=2. Here f is the fractal dimensionality of the Levy flight. For deposition with transfer of the deposited particle (site) to the nearest local minimum (deposition with restructuring), $\alpha$=(1/2(1+f) for 0<=f<=1 and $\alpha$=1 for f{\textgreater}1. For deposition onto a plane the uncertainties are much larger, but our results suggest that $\alpha$?0.36 for f<=1 and that $\alpha$ grows linearly to a value of about (1/2 for f in the range 1<=f<=2. For deposition onto a plane with restructuring, $\alpha$?f/2. All of our simulation results are consistent with the scaling relationships $\alpha$+$\alpha$/$\beta$=2 for deposition without restructuring and $\alpha$/$\beta$=2 for deposition with restructuring. Overall, our results are in good agreement with the theoretical predictions of Medina et al. [Phys. Rev. A 39, 3053 (1989)].},
